# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether this daemon runs on a node; applying it is symlink
# manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Starting it is the
# node's post-transaction hook's job, which is where a running system is available.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who enabled this unit keeps it enabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly switch it back off.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-auth-anchor.service > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-auth-anchor is installed and switched OFF. A cluster holds its accounts in one place, so
  which machine is the anchor is a decision rather than a default — installing the package claims
  nothing.

  To make this machine the anchor:

      systemctl enable --now kgsm-auth-anchor.service

  On its first start it generates the key every session in the cluster is signed with, at
  /var/lib/kgsm-auth-anchor/session-signing.pem, and publishes the public half to
  /var/lib/kgsm/cluster/auth-public-key.json for the other members on this machine. That key is
  never shipped by any package and replacing it invalidates every session that exists — back it up
  with the account store, not separately from it.

  Two things it needs from you: the browser origins allowed to sign in here
  (Anchor__AllowedOrigins in /etc/kgsm-auth-anchor/kgsm-auth-anchor.env), and — for a machine
  joining a cluster — the shared secret in /etc/kgsm/kgsm-cluster.env.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-auth-anchor/kgsm-auth-anchor.env, merge it — new
  settings are declared there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
